---
// New page, AEO sprint 2026-10-28. Answers "what does the minimum elevation for a ramp
// actually mean", which until now was a single FAQ line on the Lake Powell boat ramps page.
// The ramp table and every minimum elevation come from src/data/powell-ramps.json, whose
// source is the NPS Glen Canyon changing-lake-levels table.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';
import { breadcrumbLd, absUrl, ORG_ID } from '../../lib/seo';
import { getReading, fmtNum, powellRamps, powellRampsAsOf, POWELL_RAMPS_SOURCE } from '../../lib/lakes';

const PATH = '/guides/boat-ramp-minimum-elevation/';
const title = 'What a boat ramp minimum elevation means, and what it does not';
const description =
  'A ramp minimum elevation is the lake surface at which the bottom end of a concrete ramp leaves the water. It is not a closure notice and not a guarantee. Lake Powell ramps, with the published minimums and the live comparison.';
const UPDATED = 'October 28, 2026';

const ramps = powellRamps();
const asOf = powellRampsAsOf();
const elev = getReading('lake-powell')?.level_ft ?? null;
const lowestMin = ramps.length ? Math.min(...ramps.map((r) => r.minFt)) : null;
const highestMin = ramps.length ? Math.max(...ramps.map((r) => r.minFt)) : null;
const usable = elev == null ? [] : ramps.filter((r) => elev >= r.minFt);

const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `${absUrl(PATH)}#article`,
  headline: title,
  description,
  url: absUrl(PATH),
  mainEntityOfPage: absUrl(PATH),
  datePublished: '2026-10-28',
  dateModified: '2026-10-28',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Michael', url: absUrl('/about/') },
  publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
};
const ld = [articleLd, breadcrumbLd([
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Boat ramp minimum elevation', path: PATH },
])];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={PATH} jsonLd={ld}>
  <div class="container">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>What a boat ramp minimum elevation means</h1>
      <p>
        A ramp minimum elevation is one number per ramp: the lake surface elevation at which the bottom
        end of that ramp stops reaching the water. It is a property of the ramp's length and slope, not
        of the lake. Two ramps on the same reservoir can differ by tens of feet, which is why a lake
        that is "closed" for one ramp can still be open at another.
      </p>

      <h2 id="reading">How to read the number</h2>
      <p>
        If the lake surface is above a ramp's minimum elevation, the ramp's concrete still runs into
        the water and the question becomes one of debris, slope and whether the operator has opened it.
        If the surface is below that minimum, the ramp ends in mud and no vehicle should be on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The number is a threshold, not a promise. A ramp can be shut at a level well above its minimum
        because of storm damage, a debris line, or a rule the agency applies for the season. Always read
        the agency notice next to the number.
      </p>

      <h2 id="powell">Lake Powell, in aggregate</h2>
      <p>
        Each Glen Canyon ramp carries its own minimum lake elevation, published by the Park Service.
        This site holds that table, read on {asOf ?? 'an unknown date'}, and compares the live USGS
        surface elevation against every row at build time. Current surface:
        {elev == null ? 'no reading.' : ` ${fmtNum(elev, 2)} ft.`}
      </p>
      <div class="def-table">
        <div class="row">
          <div class="k">Ramps with a published minimum</div>
          <div class="vv">{ramps.length}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <div class="k">Lowest published minimum</div>
          <div class="vv">{lowestMin == null ? 'not published' : `${fmtNum(lowestMin, 0)} ft`}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <div class="k">Highest published minimum</div>
          <div class="vv">{highestMin == null ? 'not published' : `${fmtNum(highestMin, 0)} ft`}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <div class="k">Clearing their own minimum right now</div>
          <div class="vv">{elev == null ? 'no reading' : `${usable.length} of ${ramps.length}`}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mut" style="font-size:12px;">
        Source: <a href={POWELL_RAMPS_SOURCE} rel="nofollow noopener">NPS Glen Canyon, changing lake levels</a>.
        The row-by-row table with the agency status for each ramp is on the
        <a href="/lake/lake-powell/boat-ramps/">Lake Powell boat ramp page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="spread">Why the spread between ramps is useful</h2>
      <p>
        At Lake Powell the published minimums run from {fmtNum(lowestMin, 0)} ft to
        {fmtNum(highestMin, 0)} ft, a spread of {fmtNum(highestMin != null && lowestMin != null ? highestMin - lowestMin : null, 0)} ft.
        That spread is the difference between "the lake is too low to launch" and "the lake is too low
        to launch here". A boater who reads only the lake-wide headline loses the ramps that are still
        in the water.
      </p>
      <p>
        {elev == null
          ? 'No live reading is available, so no ramp can be ranked against its minimum right now.'
          : `At the current surface, ${usable.length} of ${ramps.length} listed ramps sit at or above their published minimum elevation.`}
        The <a href="/lake/lake-powell/boat-ramps/">Lake Powell boat ramp page</a> carries the same table
        with the agency notes, and the <a href="/lake/lake-powell/depth/">depth page</a> shows how far
        the surface is above the dam's own operating floors.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are comparing this number with another site's, check the datum first:
        <a href="/guides/why-lake-elevations-differ/">why two sites show a different lake elevation</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mut" style="font-size:12px;">Updated {UPDATED}.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
